<!--Post: 72--><!--Time: 1085388399-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST72"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ohenry<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ohenry<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, May 24, 2004 - 1:46 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I still believe that this phone call &#40;almost certainly placed by BTK&#41; is one of the biggest clues available. It has also been woefully underutilized. Here we have THE ACTUAL VOICE of a known serial killer who has eluded detection for three decades. I have complete respect for WPD, but they blew this one.  <BR> <BR>Shortly after the letter to the Eagle in March re-opened the investigation, national media flocked to Wichita. In this age of 24 hour news channels, the media can be an investigation&#39;s best friend &#40;see D.C. sniper, where Chief Moose utilized the media almost daily in an attempt to learn the identity of the sniper&#41;. The public fascination with this case is undeniable, so of course the national media was interested. Curiously, the WPD, instead of welcoming the potential help, chose instead to announce that they would not give updates to the media, nor would they disclose information. <BR> <BR>Which brings us back to topic, the Fox phone call. Two years after the call, investigators released it to the local media, which played it for a short time before it was shelved. This was a grave mistake. Somebody on the force should have been trumpeting this call nationally &#40;morning talk shows, such as GMA, which were extremely popular during that time&#41;. The more people that heard the call, the more likely that ONE &#40;which is all it would have taken&#41; would unmistakenly recognize the voice. Even today, I think this remains a potentially valuable clue which, considering the dearth of clues in the case, is all the more reason to nationally utilize this tape. It would today reach literally MILLIONS of people. Again, all it takes is one... <BR> <BR>Incidentally, much speculation exists as to whether or not BTK has disguised his voice for this call. I subscribe to the theory that he non-mechanically &#40;no devices&#41; tried to make it sound a little different. I picture him in the phone booth reading the info off Fox&#39;s stolen driver&#39;s license to the dispatcher. I&#39;m sure he practiced many times in this &#39;other voice&#39;. He knew EXACTLY what he would say. Only he didn&#39;t count on the dispatcher not understanding the address. After the second dispatcher correctly relays it to the first, BTK, caught off guard I&#39;m sure, says three words that he never practiced, because he never knew he would say them: &#34;That is correct.&#34; I believe it is within these three words that the actual, true voice comes out. <BR> <BR>Again, I respect the WPD, but to virtually ignore this potentially case-breaking evidence is highly neglectful. And as for deciding to continue withholding evidence from the media/public, after nearly 1/3 of a century, perhaps it is time to revisit that strategy. Thanx, -O&#39;Henry<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 72-->
<!--Post: 81--><!--Time: 1085510359-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST81"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:webmaster@catchbtk.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tom_voigt<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tom_voigt<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 25, 2004 - 11:39 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ohenry, I would think at a public phone booth it would be hard to use a device to disguise your voice without raising suspicions. Certainly it&#39;s possible, but probably not with anything mechanical. <BR> <BR>In any event, I&#39;m most interested in the rather unique way BTK pronounces his words. He sounds very much like a teacher of some kind, IMHO.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 81-->
<!--Post: 84--><!--Time: 1085526992-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST84"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Scream187<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Scream187<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 25, 2004 - 4:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ohenry: i like that theory about the &#34;that is correct.&#34; Actually i like it so much that I am the one that came up with it a while back... <BR> <BR>oh yea, and when i got a little more time ill explain why the witness testimony &#40;the fireman that witnessed the phone call&#41; is a little weird... before someone takes credit for that too...<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 84-->
<!--Post: 86--><!--Time: 1085538455-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST86"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lilahsue<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lilahsue<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, May 25, 2004 - 7:27 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I haven&#39;t read your theory Screm187.  Is it on this site somewhere?  I know I did read somewhere &#40;possibly posted by you&#41; that it was weird that the fireman didn&#39;t hang up the phone...or didn&#39;t use the phone I guess.  I did think it was a very good thought.  Is this what you are referring to?  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 86-->
<!--Post: 98--><!--Time: 1085563321-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST98"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ohenry<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ohenry<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, May 26, 2004 - 2:22 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Hey, scream. Glad you came up with that theory. When did this occur? I&#39;ve held firm to this theory since, oh, probably 1979. I certainly can&#39;t say I&#39;ve seen you on here before, so how would I have &#39;stolen&#39; your theory?  <BR> <BR>Some conclusions on the case can be drawn by more than one person who have not previously discussed the case. By no means did I ever think I was the only one who thought about &#39;that is correct&#39; being an unguarded statement and a possible key. I can&#39;t imagine that you had delusions of being the only one either.  <BR> <BR>By the way, I have followed this case for YEARS and am quite capable of forming my own opinions. I don&#39;t want to argue, because this is too good a site to stoop to that, but I will give you a little advice. In the future, if you are going to accuse others of taking your ideas, you might want to actually POST those ideas first. Otherwise, I would have had to somehow &#39;gotten in your mind&#39; to get &#39;your&#39; ideas. Since I have never even heard of you before, I can assure you that isn&#39;t the case. <BR> <BR>Have a great day! -O&#39;Henry &#40;I stole that name idea from an author and a candy bar&#41;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 98-->
<!--Post: 164--><!--Time: 1086258397-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST164"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Unsub<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Unsub<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, June 03, 2004 - 3:26 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here are my thoughts on the call. To me it sounds as though he may have a New England area accent, something about the way he pronounces the o in Fox. I also think he is ex-military as the first time I heard it I thought he was spelling something out&#40;like Able,Baker,Charlie&#41;military style. <BR> <BR>I think it is quite posible he used a voice device to modulate the tone. You can get these on the web today for 25 bucks or so. I understand they would have been less avaiable back then and probably bulkier but I am sure they were around. May have been sold as a joke/novelty item&#40;Fool your frineds into thinking you are a woman!!&#41;. Also BTK seems to be quite the technical kinda guy. I would be interested to know if any of those electronic kits they used to sell had such a device as one of the 101 projects you could make. Maybe plans wre available via mail order from the classifieds in Popular Mechaincs, I recall getting plans for a still from there in my youth.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 164-->
<!--Post: 256--><!--Time: 1086965994-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST256"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jenkins<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jenkins<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, June 11, 2004 - 7:59 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This post relates to the phone being off the hook. <BR> <BR>I have read here, and on other forums, suspicion expressed, in regards to the phone being off the hook. Why didn&#39;t the fireman hang it up? Why didnt he make his call? <BR> <BR> <BR>Nobody ever said he didn&#39;t hang it up. Nobody ever said he didnt make his call. <BR> <BR> <BR>Here is what I presume happened. <BR> <BR>Fireman goes into get his change &#40;as he said in his statement&#41; <BR> <BR>He sees BTK walking off and the phone off the hook. <BR> <BR>Seeing the phone hanging off the hook was noteworthy enough for him to recall when questioned moments later by the police. <BR> <BR>I imagine after he got his change he did go over use the phone and then hang it up after done. <BR> <BR>Then once the police got there and asked him about the man who just used the phone, he said I came to make a call, realized I had no change, went into the gas station got my change and came out and saw the phone hanging from its cord and the man who was using it prior walking off that way. <BR> <BR>That I believe puts to rest suspicion as to why the fireman didn&#39;t make his call. I&#39;m sure he did. <BR> <BR> <BR>BUT, maybe the fireman was BTK. Maybe he called the police, went into the gas station got some candy lol and some more change went out made another call, and when the police got there he told of having arrived seeing a man on the phone, then he went in to get some change came out and saw the phone dangling and a man walking off. <BR> <BR>I doubt that though.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 256-->
<!--Post: 1435--><!--Time: 1101213480-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1435"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, November 23, 2004 - 4:38 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->BTK may have left the phone off the hook so the police could be sure and trace the location of the call. Tracing a call was much different then than now. I know someone in the mid &#39;70s who had an obscene caller caught because a telephone employee just happened to be physically present at some switching station when one of the calls came in. At that point in time, that is the only way tracing could be accomplished. It was a hands-on method. I don&#39;t think that the phones hung up automatically either. If party A did not hang up, party B&#39;s phoneline would remain open as well, thus preventing them from  making any other calls. A common practice if one was getting obnoxious calls! <BR> <BR>Perhaps BTK was in a position to be savvy to the technology to some degree or another. He seems to be such a well rounded person in so many areas: a jack of  all trades.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1435-->
<!--Post: 1441--><!--Time: 1101261768-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1441"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, November 23, 2004 - 6:02 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Interesting observations Teardrop. I also remember the old &#34;party lines&#34; where multiple families shared one phone line.  .  .  a rather nasty way to communicate with privacy at a minimum <BR> <BR>If BTK is a well rounded person with many areas of knowledge it would not be surprising. BTK has copied many of the Zodiac&#39;s traits. In so doing he may have studied many of the Zodiac&#39;s skills used so &#34;successfully&#34; if you can call it that in Northern California.  .  .  do you also see the similarities between the two ?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1441-->
<!--Post: 1443--><!--Time: 1101309770-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST1443"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, November 24, 2004 - 7:22 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Yes, Tac, I agree. He appears to have studied quite a number of killers and learned many lessons from them. How well I remember the party lines: what you could learn! Everything you did not need to know. <BR> <BR>Like psych majors who go into psychology to learn about themselves &#40;of which I was one&#41;, I would venture to say that his initial study was to learn what was happening to him. Compulsions do have demonic force in a life, despite the white knuckles of the individual trying to hold them at bay. <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s lament about going to mental health professionals and telling them about his compulsions to kill would indicate that perhaps at one time he was indeed trying to hold on for dear life. Charles Whitman&#39;s psychiatrist did not take him seriously about his urge to go up into the UT tower and shoot people. BTK may not have been taken seriously either. I lived in a town where a man walked every evening to the police station and spent the night there because he was afraid he was going to kill someone. At least he was proactive in his life. <BR> <BR>The Zodiac and BTK have the same cunning and blood lust. I do think that BTK is more intelligent, is multifaceted in his skills in terms of areas of proficiency related to employment and personal pursuits &#40;hobbies, talents&#41;, and is more social and less isolated than is thought of most serial killers. He may even attend church. Only 7% of serial killers have been observed going to church. <BR> <BR>Maintaining a double persona requires huge amounts of energy, which will eventually run him to exaustion. His penchant as a &#39;neat freak&#39; is one way to remain in control. <BR> <BR>The saddest part for BTK is that he can never fully share his heart with someone, no matter how much they love him. He can never feel safe and secure in someone&#39;s arms. And that secret place must be the lonliest of all: quiet desparation in which he is truly a dead man walking.   <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 1443-->
<!--Post: 3075--><!--Time: 1104427399-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3075"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 9:23 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I agree with &#34;UNSUB&#34; that BLTs&#39; voice has a &#39;New England&#39; accent. <BR> <BR>I&#39;ve got it! It&#39;s TED KENNEDY! Just kidding. &#40;Now don&#39;t all you Rush Windbag fans come chiming in &amp; agreeing!&#41;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3075-->
<!--Post: 3123--><!--Time: 1104470205-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3123"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Q<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Q<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, December 30, 2004 - 9:16 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->IMO: Not a New England but possibly a Pittsburgh accent<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3123-->
<!--Post: 3162--><!--Time: 1104534348-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST3162"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Bargle<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bargle<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, December 31, 2004 - 3:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->My thought, it may be that BTK was trying as best he could to speak in an unaccented manner. Whether it was to disguise his voice or simply trying be sure he would be understood, I can&#39;t say. Given that the operator couldn&#39;t understand him, it didn&#39;t work too well.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 3162-->
<!--Post: 4018--><!--Time: 1105539184-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4018"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beck<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beck<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 6:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t hear an accent with this phone call, I agree with Bargle, I think he was trying to speak without an accent. There is something that caught my attention. The way he says &#34;Nancy Fox&#34;. Is it just me, or does it sound almost robotic, like an old Speak &amp; Spell &#40;remember those&#41; would have said it? With it being a phone booth and all, I doubt he used one, but maybe he was trying to make his voice sound that way.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4018-->
<!--Post: 4021--><!--Time: 1105540226-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4021"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 6:30 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Beck!  Thank you very much.  It&#39;s been driving me crazy trying to remember what the name of that was.  A &#34; Speak &amp; Spell.&#34;  That&#39;s it.  My little sister had one in  the 70&#39;s and yes, that&#39;s &#42;exactly&#42; what BTK sounds like to me on that recording.  Exactly. <BR> <BR>Does anyone here have an old Speak &amp; Spell they could check out?  Make it say Nancy Fox&#39;s name and I&#39;ll guarantee you, it will sound like that recording. <BR> <BR>Now, this could mess the S&amp;S theory up, but wouldn&#39;t the witness to BTK on the pay phone have noticed that the guy had a Speak &amp; Spell?  They weren&#39;t really big, but they weren&#39;t small, either and I think they were orange and yellow.  The only way this could work would be if the caller &#40;BTK&#41; had his back to the witness, the witness may not have been paying much attention anyway or that wasn&#39;t BTK the witness saw.  Or, for all we know, maybe he did see a S&amp;S, told the police that and the police just aren&#39;t releasing that info?  I don&#39;t know why they wouldn&#39;t, but...just thinking. <BR> <BR>So, can anyone out there get their hands on a old Speak &amp; Spell?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4021-->
<!--Post: 4026--><!--Time: 1105544983-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4026"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Wade<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Wade<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 7:49 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->He didn&#39;t use a speak and spell.  He would have to enter everything he said....the answer came to quickly on the phone for him to do that.  I don&#39;t like to bash other peoples ideas but thats kind of absurd. <BR>Even if he did use one....what would that have to do with solving the case???  I think its very irrelevant.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4026-->
<!--Post: 4033--><!--Time: 1105554007-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4033"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 10:20 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Bargle nailed it. The thing about an accent is that depending what part of the country one is from it&#39;s all relative. To me the voice sounds exactly like a fairly elderly man I used to speak with by phone at work. The man grew up in Hibbing, Minnesota, lives in California, but his accent remained a thick &#34;upper Midwest&#34; <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4033-->
<!--Post: 4034--><!--Time: 1105554300-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4034"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 10:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t think anyone said either way if it&#39;s relevant, Wade, and I don&#39;t think it&#39;s absurd at all.  BTK&#39;s voice sounds very mechanical to me in that recording and reminds me very strongly of how a Speak &amp; Spell used to sound. <BR> <BR>As far as entering it in, he wouldn&#39;t have had to do that at the phone, especially if he&#39;d planned his crime ahead of time.  There were tape recorders then and many have speculated that the audio released has been edited, so we don&#39;t know how long his responses may have actually taken.   <BR> <BR>Does all this talk mean he actually used a Speak &amp; Spell?  No.  Does the fact that he didn&#39;t use a Speak &amp; Spell negate the importance of his voice sounding mechanically reproduced to some?  No. <BR> <BR>Looking into it, Speak &amp; Spells don&#39;t seem to have been released until 1978 and the call was made in 1977, if I&#39;m remembering correctly, so that effectively rules that out, but that doesn&#39;t change the interesting tidbit &#40;to me anyway&#41; that his voice doesn&#39;t sound quite genuinely human to me. <BR> <BR>What else could he have used at the time to make his voice sound that way or was he purposefully trying to sound that way? <BR> <BR>Is it important? Well, how would we know? <BR> <BR>Do I think this is the big break that&#39;s going to crack the case wide open?  Uh, no. <BR> <BR>It is simply an interesting observation and my suggestion that someone try the word &#34;Fox&#34; on a Speak &amp; Spell was to demonstrate how artificial it does sound in the recording.  &#34;Correct&#34; also sounds very artificial. <BR> <BR>Jeez.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4034-->
<!--Post: 4038--><!--Time: 1105557436-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4038"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Freya<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Freya<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 11:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I was suprised to read the opinions that his voice on the 911 call sounded North Eastern. Weird as this may seem, I think he sounds almost exactly like my ex-boyfriend, who is a cop in Kansas City. Anyway, I&#39;m from Texas, so when i heard the 911 call, I immediately thought, &#34;Wow, he sounds just like &#60;blank&#62;, so that must be what a midwestern accent sounds like.&#34; The cadence and formality of his statements sounded like the way a police officer or military personnel would speak. That, too, is likely why they sounded so similar.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4038-->
<!--Post: 4042--><!--Time: 1105559734-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4042"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Beck<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Beck<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 11:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wade, You&#39;re absolutely right, if he were to have used a S &amp; S, he literally would have had to type in a word, hit the enter button, then type the next word he wanted to say. But what if BTK was trying to make his voice sound that way, maybe to cover a thick accent. I agree with Jujuree that this is not going to be the tidbit to land him in jail. It was just something that I noticed. <BR> <BR>Here&#39;s a link to a S &amp; S emulator, you cant get it to say what you want it to, it&#39;s pre-programed, but you can get the idea of what it sounded like.  <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.speaknspell.co.uk/" target=_top>http://www.speaknspell.co.uk/</a> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4042-->
<!--Post: 4055--><!--Time: 1105585715-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4055"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Jujuree<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Jujuree<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, January 12, 2005 - 7:08 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I found that simulator, too, Beck.  LOL, I wish it had more on it, I enjoyed playing around with it for a few minutes.  &#40;Also, now I feel old!&#41; <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 4055-->
